Heap: it is complete binary tree follows special rule called the Heap Property.

types:
-> Min Heap : Smallest element aleways at the root.
-> Max Heap : Largest element aleways at the root.

Why do we use Heap?
-> suppose you have
    50,10,80,40,20
    someone asks:
      -> give the Largest element quickly
    
    Array --> 0(n)
    Heap -->  0(1)

    that's why Heaps are used for:
    -> Top K Largest elements
    -> Kth Largest/Smallest
    -> Priority scheduling
    -> Dijkstra Algorithm
    -> Huffman Coding
    -> Median of Data Stream

Min Heap: 
  Java has built in class: 

     PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(); //this is min heap default.

     //Important Methods: 
     --> pq.add(10); //insert element
     --> pq.offer(); //insert element
     --> pq.peek(); //Smallest element
     --> pq.poll(); //removes and returns smallest element
     --> pq.size(); 
     --> pq.isEmpty();

Max Heap: 
 By default PriorityQueue is a Min Heap.

 To create a Max Heap, use:
   
   PriorityQueue<Integer> maxHeap = new PriorityQueue<>(Collection.reverseOrder());

⌚ Time Complexity (Very Important)

   Operation      Time
-> add()          0(logn)
-> offer()        0(logn)
-> poll()         0(logn)
-> peek()         0(1)
-> size()         0(1)
-> isEmpty()      0(1)      
   